  "worked_solution": "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcosh}\u2061u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $10$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{4}^{10}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{10}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcosh}\u2061u\\,{|}_{4}^{10} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcosh}\u2061(10)-\\operatorname{arcosh}\u2061(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10+\\sqrt{{(10)}^{2}-1})-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4+\\sqrt{{(4)}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10+\\sqrt{99})-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4+\\sqrt{15}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{10+\\sqrt{99}}{4+\\sqrt{15}})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
